<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic PUBLIC "-//OASIS//DTD DITA Topic//EN" "http://docs.oasis-open.org/dita/v1.1/OS/dtd/topic.dtd">
<topic id="tpc_context" xmlns:ditaarch="http://dita.oasis-open.org/architecture/2005/">
    <title>Context</title>
    <body>
        #if($configParams.getCustomParams().get("document.context.intro"))
        <p>$configParams.getCustomParams().get("document.context.intro")</p>
        #else
        <p>$utils.getText($configParams.getCustomParams().get("document.context.intro.key"))</p>
        #end

        <section>
            <title>Vulnerability Severity Metrics</title>
            <p id="scoring">
                Generally, vulnerability severity is measured using the <abbreviated-form keyref="cvss"/>.
                Currently, three versions (2.0, 3.1 and 4.0) of the CVSS scoring system are commonly applied and
                supported by the <abbreviated-form keyref="nvd"/>.
            </p>
            <p id="scoring-metrics">
                A vulnerability may carry several CVSS vectors in different version. Since the more recent versions of
                CVSS convey more details with respect to scoring the vulnerability, the available vector in the latest
                CVSS version is used as primary reference. The metrics computed from the primary vector are used for
                score computing, severity categorization, ordering and charting.
            </p>
            <p>
                <!-- https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln-metrics/cvss; https://www.first.org/cvss/v3.1/specification-document -->
                The report uses the most recent CVSS severity scheme as defined in the CVSS 3.1 and CVSS 4.0 specifications:
                <table id="severity_scheme">
                    <title>CVSS Severity Scheme</title>
                    <tgroup cols="5">
                        <colspec colname="COLSPEC0" colnum="1" colwidth="15*" />
                        <colspec colname="COLSPEC1" colnum="2" colwidth="20*" />
                        <colspec colname="COLSPEC2" colnum="3" colwidth="65*" />
                        <thead>
                            <row>
                                <entry colname="COLSPEC0" valign="middle">Severity Rating</entry>
                                <entry colname="COLSPEC1" valign="middle">CVSS Severity Score</entry>
                                <entry colname="COLSPEC2" valign="middle">Remarks</entry>
                            </row>
                        </thead>

                        <tbody>
                            <row>
                                <entry valign="middle"><svg-container><svg:svg xmlns:svg="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="56px" height="17px">
                                    <svg:rect x="0" y="0" rx="3" ry="3" width="56" height="17" style="fill:#cacecf" />
                                    <svg:text text-anchor="middle" alignment-baseline="central" fill="black">
                                        <svg:tspan x="28" y="12" font-weight="bold">None</svg:tspan>
                                    </svg:text>
                                </svg:svg></svg-container></entry>
                                <entry valign="middle">0.0</entry>
                                <entry valign="middle">In the CVSS 2.0 specification 0.0 is included in severity rating Low.</entry>
                            </row>
                            <row>
                                <entry valign="middle"><svg-container><svg:svg xmlns:svg="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="56px" height="17px">
                                    <svg:rect x="0" y="0" rx="3" ry="3" width="56" height="17" style="fill:#ffcc00" />
                                    <svg:text text-anchor="middle" alignment-baseline="central" fill="black">
                                        <svg:tspan x="28" y="12" font-weight="bold">Low</svg:tspan>
                                    </svg:text>
                                </svg:svg></svg-container></entry>
                                <entry valign="middle">0.1 - 3.9</entry>
                                <entry valign="middle">&nbsp;</entry>
                            </row>
                            <row>
                                <entry valign="middle"><svg-container><svg:svg xmlns:svg="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="56px" height="17px">
                                    <svg:rect x="0" y="0" rx="3" ry="3" width="56" height="17" style="fill:#ff9b05" />
                                    <svg:text text-anchor="middle" alignment-baseline="central" fill="black">
                                        <svg:tspan x="28" y="12" font-weight="bold">Medium</svg:tspan>
                                    </svg:text>
                                </svg:svg></svg-container></entry>
                                <entry valign="middle">4.0 - 6.9</entry>
                                <entry valign="middle">&nbsp;</entry>
                            </row>
                            <row>
                                <entry valign="middle"><svg-container><svg:svg xmlns:svg="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="56px" height="17px">
                                    <svg:rect x="0" y="0" rx="3" ry="3" width="56" height="17" style="fill:#ff5a0e" />
                                    <svg:text text-anchor="middle" alignment-baseline="central" fill="white">
                                        <svg:tspan x="28" y="12" font-weight="bold">High</svg:tspan>
                                    </svg:text>
                                </svg:svg></svg-container></entry>
                                <entry valign="middle">7.0 - 8.9</entry>
                                <entry valign="middle">In the CVSS 2.0 specification the severity rating Critical does not exist. CVSS scores from 7.0 to 10.0 are
                                    all rated as High.
                                </entry>
                            </row>
                            <row>
                                <entry valign="middle"><svg-container><svg:svg xmlns:svg="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="56px" height="17px">
                                    <svg:rect x="0" y="0" rx="3" ry="3" width="56" height="17" style="fill:#ff172d" />
                                    <svg:text text-anchor="middle" alignment-baseline="central" fill="white">
                                        <svg:tspan x="28" y="12" font-weight="bold">Critical</svg:tspan>
                                    </svg:text>
                                </svg:svg></svg-container></entry>
                                <entry valign="middle">9.0 - 10.0</entry>
                                <entry valign="middle">&nbsp;</entry>
                            </row>
                        </tbody>
                    </tgroup>
                </table>
            </p>
            #if($configParams.getCustomParams().get("document.context.metrics.epss.enabled") == 'true')
            <p>
                An alternative scoring system for vulnerabilities is the <abbreviated-form keyref="epss"/>. The EPSS system
                provides an exploit probability (as value between 0.0 and 1.0) for a vulnerability within the
                next 30 days. This report documents the available EPSS scores within the vulnerability priority section,
                combining them with other metrics like <abbreviated-form keyref="kev"/>.
            </p>
            #end
        </section>

        #if($configParams.getCustomParams().get("document.context.assessment.status.enabled") == 'true')
        <section>
            <title>Vulnerability Assessment Status</title>
            <p id="status">
                At the individual vulnerability level, within a specific usage, integration, and deployment context, the
                following assessment statuses can be determined:
            <ul>
                <li><b>Applicable</b> - The vulnerability is present and potentially exploitable within the scope of
                    the asset. Specifically, there is no justification or evidence to prove that the vulnerability cannot
                    be exploited.
                </li>
                <li><b>Not Applicable</b> - The vulnerability is considered not applicable if evidence can be provided
                    showing that it is neither accessible nor reachable within the asset's context. This typically applies
                    to vulnerabilities in unused or deactivated functions and interfaces.
                </li>
                <li><b>Insignificant</b> - A vulnerability is regarded as insignificant if its original severity
                    does not exceed a predetermined score, or if it has been assessed and explicitly marked as insignificant
                    in the given context. This status helps prioritize assessments while still providing a comprehensive view.
                </li>
                <li><b>Void</b> - A vulnerability is considered void if it was reported and assessed for other versions
                    of the asset but affects component versions not included in the current asset version. The affected
                    components have either been patched or removed. These are reported to provide continuity over the
                    product's life cycle.
                </li>
                <li><b>In Review</b> - A vulnerability is placed in review when assessment details are insufficient
                    or currently unavailable.
                </li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </section>
        #end

        #if($configParams.getCustomParams().get("document.context.remediation.enabled") == 'true')
        <section>
            <title>Vulnerability Remediation</title>
            <p id="remediation">
                Vulnerability assessments are performed to guide the remediation of vulnerabilities in a given context.
                During an assessment, it is possible to document a rationale justifying the assessment status and to add
                further details. Specifically:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    The CVSS vector may be adjusted to reflect the integration or deployment context. This may
                    result in a context-specific severity score (Score<sub>ctx</sub>) that deviates from the initial score.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Identified risks can be documented and disclosed.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Measures can be defined to mitigate these risks.
                </li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </section>
        #end

        #if($configParams.getCustomParams().get("document.context.prioritization.enabled") == 'true')
        <section>
            <title>Vulnerability Prioritization</title>
            <p>
                The Vulnerability Priority Score automates the ranking of vulnerabilities based on their relevance
                within a specific context.The score is computed using the contextualized CVSS score as a base score, with
                additional metrics applied. These additional metrics are detailed in the priority sections of the vulnerability
                details chapters. A priority label is derived for each vulnerability, indicating whether, due to an elevated
                Priority Score, timely treatment is required.
            </p>
            <p>
                The Vulnerability Priority Score covers different aspects related to vulnerable software components or
                the vulnerability itself. Specifically, the priority score computation takes <abbreviated-form keyref="epss"/>,
                <abbreviated-form keyref="kev"/>, and Software Life Cycle details into account.
            </p>
            <p>
                The Priority Score is a non-normalized, non-continuous metric. It is not capped, in order to
                provide a relative measurement for ranking vulnerabilities using a policy-driven prioritization approach.
            </p>
            <p>
                The following table shows the priority label scheme:
            <table id="priority_scheme">
                <title>Priority Scheme</title>
                <tgroup cols="5">
                    <colspec colname="COLSPEC0" colnum="1" colwidth="15*" />
                    <colspec colname="COLSPEC1" colnum="2" colwidth="20*" />
                    <colspec colname="COLSPEC2" colnum="3" colwidth="65*" />
                    <thead>
                    <row>
                        <entry colname="COLSPEC0" valign="middle">Priority Rating</entry>
                        <entry colname="COLSPEC1" valign="middle">Priority Score</entry>
                        <entry colname="COLSPEC2" valign="middle">Description</entry>
                    </row>
                    </thead>

                    <tbody>
                    <row>
                        <entry valign="middle"><svg-container><svg:svg xmlns:svg="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="56px" height="17px">
                            <svg:rect x="0" y="0" rx="3" ry="3" width="56" height="17" style="fill:#cacecf" />
                            <svg:text text-anchor="middle" alignment-baseline="central" fill="black">
                                <svg:tspan x="28" y="12" font-weight="bold">Default</svg:tspan>
                            </svg:text>
                        </svg:svg></svg-container></entry>
                        <entry valign="middle">0.0</entry>
                        <entry valign="middle">No raised priority.</entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <entry valign="middle"><svg-container><svg:svg xmlns:svg="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="56px" height="17px">
                            <svg:rect x="0" y="0" rx="3" ry="3" width="56" height="17" style="fill:#ff9b05" />
                            <svg:text text-anchor="middle" alignment-baseline="central" fill="black">
                                <svg:tspan x="28" y="12" font-weight="bold">Elevated</svg:tspan>
                            </svg:text>
                        </svg:svg></svg-container></entry>
                        <entry valign="middle">0.1 - 6.9</entry>
                        <entry valign="middle">The priority is elevated. An assessment is recommended.</entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <entry valign="middle"><svg-container><svg:svg xmlns:svg="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="56px" height="17px">
                            <svg:rect x="0" y="0" rx="3" ry="3" width="56" height="17" style="fill:#ff5a0e" />
                            <svg:text text-anchor="middle" alignment-baseline="central" fill="white">
                                <svg:tspan x="28" y="12" font-weight="bold">Due</svg:tspan>
                            </svg:text>
                        </svg:svg></svg-container></entry>
                        <entry valign="middle">7.0 - 8.9</entry>
                        <entry valign="middle">This priority level requires planned assessment activities.</entry>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <entry valign="middle"><svg-container><svg:svg xmlns:svg="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="56px" height="17px">
                            <svg:rect x="0" y="0" rx="3" ry="3" width="56" height="17" style="fill:#ff172d" />
                            <svg:text text-anchor="middle" alignment-baseline="central" fill="white">
                                <svg:tspan x="28" y="12" font-weight="bold">Escalate</svg:tspan>
                            </svg:text>
                        </svg:svg></svg-container></entry>
                        <entry valign="middle">9.0 or &gt;9.0</entry>
                        <entry valign="middle">The vulnerability is escalated, requiring immediate coordination of activities.</entry>
                    </row>
                    </tbody>
                </tgroup>
            </table>
            </p>
        </section>
        #end

        #if($configParams.getCustomParams().get("document.context.exploitability.enabled") == 'true')
        <section>
            <title>Vulnerability Exploitability</title>
            <p>
                There are three distinct sources for exploitability information:
            <ul>
                <li><abbreviated-form keyref="epss"/> - Provides a probability score estimating the likelihood of a vulnerability being exploited in the next 30 days.</li>
                <li><abbreviated-form keyref="cvss"/> - Includes various "Exploitability" (E) metrics across different versions that indicate the current state of exploit techniques or code availability.</li>
                <li><abbreviated-form keyref="kev"/> - A catalog of vulnerabilities that have been confirmed to be exploited in the wild.</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                While the Vulnerability Priority Score evaluates most of these sources, it also incorporates additional
                factors such as the component lifecycle (end-of-life dates), context assessments, and specific keywords.
                To provide a focused view, the Exploitability label isolates the exploitability metrics. It utilizes a
                worst-case ruleset, evaluating a prioritized list of rules to assign the highest applicable exploitability label
                to a vulnerability. These rules assess conditions such as minimum and maximum EPSS probabilities, specific
                CVSS vector metrics, and the presence of KEV or ransomware campaign entries.
            </p>
            <p>
                By default, the exploitability labels are assigned as follows:
            <ul>
                <li><b>Evident</b> - Assigned when there is clear evidence of an exploit from at least one source. Criteria include a KEV entry, an EPSS probability greater than 80%, or specific CVSS metrics (e.g., CVSS 4.0 <code>AU:Y</code>, <code>E:A</code>, or <code>E:H</code> in previous CVSS versions).</li>
                <li><b>Unconfirmed</b> - Assigned when there is some indication of exploit information. This includes an EPSS probability between 10% and 80%, or CVSS exploitability vector metrics such as <code>E:P</code> (Proof-of-Concept) or <code>E:F</code> (Functional), but no concrete evidence of an active attack.</li>
                <li><b>Not Evident</b> - Assigned when exploitability data (such as EPSS or CVSS) is present, but provides no indication of an exploit.</li>
                <li><b>Undefined</b> - Assigned in case there is no EPSS, CVSS, and KEV information.</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </section>
        #end

        #if($configParams.getCustomParams().get("document.context.threshold.enabled") == 'true')
        <section>
            <title>Insignificant Vulnerabilities Threshold</title>
            <p>$utils.getText("inventory-report-vulnerability.vulnerability-threshold")</p>
        </section>
        #end
    </body>
</topic>
